sections.say:
"Sort by start, then sweep. The trap is deciding what each new event gets compared against. The instinct is `sorted[i - 1]`, the immediately previous event — and that is wrong.

Counterexample: A-101 runs 09:00–13:00, B-205 runs 10:00–10:30, C-309 runs 11:00–11:30. Sorted by start they're already A, B, C. Comparing C to B asks whether `'11:00' < '10:30'` — false, so no conflict gets reported. But C obviously collides with A, which is still running at 11:00. The `i-1` comparison misses it because B is short and nested inside A.

So I carry `last` pointing at the event with the FURTHEST end seen so far, not the most recent one:

```
const sorted = [...events].sort((a, b) =>
  a.start < b.start ? -1 : a.start > b.start ? 1 : 0
);
const conflicts = [];
let last = sorted[0];
for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {
  const curr = sorted[i];
  if (curr.start < last.end) conflicts.push([last.caseId, curr.caseId]);
  if (curr.end > last.end) last = curr;   // running max on end
}
```

The invariant is one sentence: `last.end` is the maximum end time among everything already scanned. Because the array is sorted by start, if `curr` doesn't reach back past that maximum it can't reach back past any earlier event either — so ONE comparison per event is sufficient. That's the sort-then-sweep template carrying a running max, the same skeleton as merge-intervals, meeting-rooms, and non-overlapping-intervals."

"Time is O(n log n), and the sort is the entire cost — the sweep is a single pass with O(1) work per event, so it's O(n) and vanishes into the sort. If the calendar already arrives ordered (a database query with `ORDER BY start`), drop the sort and the whole thing is O(n).

Space is O(n) for the cloned array plus O(c) for the conflicts output, where c ≤ n-1. The clone is the protect-the-caller cost and I'd keep it; if the interviewer says the input is disposable, `events.sort(...)` in place makes it O(c).

The template generalizes directly. Sort by start, sweep carrying a running aggregate:
• carry the running max end and PUSH on overlap → this problem;
• carry the running max end and MERGE on overlap → merge-intervals;
• carry a min-heap of end times → meeting-rooms-II, 'how many rooms do I need';
• sort by END instead and carry the last kept end → non-overlapping-intervals / activity selection, which is the greedy variant.

Same four lines of skeleton every time; what changes is what you carry and what you do on overlap.

At real scale this is a sweep-line over a timeline, and in production it's usually a database constraint instead — Postgres has an exclusion constraint over a `tstzrange` that refuses to insert an overlapping booking at all. Worth naming: the best version of this algorithm is the one you never have to run, because the write was rejected."

reference.code:
const depositions = [
  { caseId: 'A-101', start: '09:00', end: '10:30' },
  { caseId: 'B-205', start: '10:00', end: '11:00' },
  { caseId: 'C-309', start: '11:00', end: '12:00' },
  { caseId: 'D-412', start: '11:30', end: '13:00' }
];

function findConflicts(events) {
  const sorted = [...events].sort((a, b) =>
    a.start < b.start ? -1 : a.start > b.start ? 1 : 0
  );
  const conflicts = [];
  let last = sorted[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {
    const curr = sorted[i];
    if (curr.start < last.end) {
      conflicts.push([last.caseId, curr.caseId]);
    }
    if (curr.end > last.end) last = curr;
  }
  return conflicts;
}

console.log(JSON.stringify(findConflicts(depositions)));

